That is the basic robe effect, not what I was describing.

spl1-343.txt 409: 

[final] chance = (chance * (20-penalty) / 15) - penalty

Robes reduce "penalty", which obviously increases success rate.  However, 
when "chance" is zero (it can't go negative), the "(20-penalty) / 15)" 
part of the formula is irrelevant -- but a negative "penalty" will still 
provide a small final chance of success.  

For an example of this second effect, start a monk in wizard mode and 
wish for a "holy spellbook of cancellation", or any other high level 
book.  The fail rate will be only 88%, not the 100% most people would 
guess.

> Polymorph is great even if it only works one tenth of the time.

Sure.  Think of it as costing 300 mana per successful cast.  A typical 
midgame character[1] will take 3200 turns to regenerate this, but up on 
DL1 he can survive indefinitely against the randomly generated monsters.

If our character is a monk with the Eye (clearly the best first wish for 
a neutral monk), the increased mana regen will average him one successful 
cast every 167 turns, which is very reasonable.



[1] Non-wizard, XL 12, d2 mana regen.

